# Verify Deterministically — Never Guess Endianness/Correctness

This project has **ground truth**: `game-files/xbox-vz.wad` (BE) and
`game-files/vz.wad` (LE) are the *same base game*, so any BE→LE conversion can be
checked **byte-for-byte**. There is never a reason to judge data by plausibility.

## Banned: plausibility heuristics

Do NOT conclude data is wrong/right from "looks BE", "looks LE", "value out of
range", "float looks huge", etc. These are noise. Proof: the "looks BE"
checks in `tools/verify_ucfx_endian.py` flag **4634 issues in 400 blocks of the
retail, known-good LE `vz.wad`** — a *higher* per-block rate than the converted
patch WAD. A heuristic that fails on the shipping game proves nothing about a
patch. Mesh vertex streams (packed/compressed formats) and non-placement `flgs`
bodies trip these checks constantly even when perfectly little-endian.

If you catch yourself writing a range/magnitude test to infer endianness, stop
and use a deterministic check below instead.

## Required: deterministic verification (pick one)

1. **Rosetta byte-exact** (converter correctness): convert an
   `xbox-vz.wad` block and assert it equals the matching `vz.wad` block
   byte-for-byte (`tools/wad_be_le_oracle.py`). This is how the converter is
   proven, not by inspection.
2. **Patch == converter(source)** (patch correctness): a patch block must equal
   re-running the validated converter on the original X360 source block,
   byte-for-byte. No PC ground truth needed — even for DLC-only assets that
   never shipped on PC.
3. **Typed coverage** (no silent gaps): strict converter, `Strict swap
   failures: 0`, `Total fallback u32 ops: 0`, no blind tag sweeps. Every chunk
   tag/type must have a typed converter (see Byte-Swap Policy in `AGENTS.md`).
4. **Round-trip idempotence** where a known inverse exists.

## Trust / distrust

- TRUST: byte-exact diffs vs ground truth, `Strict swap failures`,
  `fallback u32 ops`, typed-coverage reports, the running game (x32dbg).
- DISTRUST: "endian issues" counts from plausibility scanners. Treat that number
  as noise unless it is calibrated against retail `vz.wad` and shown to be ~0
  there first.
